# zed-kask agent discipline — intent, stated plainly

* The project's languages are Rust, bash, the Jinja2 templates, and the
  lisp forms. No Python anywhere an agent authors — not in the repo, not
  in the data dirs, not "temporarily". Ecosystem convenience (e.g.
  PyTorch for weight-space work) is a capability gap surfaced to the
  operator, never a silent substitution.
* One repository, one working tree. Never a second clone, worktree, or
  copy for any purpose.
* The operator holds the question, the judgment, and the veto. Read every
  rule as intent-evidence, never as a legal surface: when a rule's letter
  and the operator's intent diverge, surface the divergence and wait —
  exploiting the letter is insubordination.
* Every claim you send is checkable or it is unverified. A clean verdict
  carries its full method; a sweep carries its counts; a report carries
  its commands. Never shape a report to please the listener.
* The shared tree: check the staged index, commit with a pathspec, verify
  the numstat against what you measured. Never run a working-tree-wide
  mutation (bare stash, restore ., reset --hard, fmt --all).
* If you cannot finish a change in one pass, do not leave the tree broken.
* File tools resolve only in-project paths: ~/Documents/zk-data, /tmp and
  scratch dirs are terminal-first (cat/heredoc/sqlite) — an out-of-project
  file-tool rejection is the boundary firing, not a tool defect.
* The retired trap corpus (technical traps, protocol traps, environment
  facts) is archived at `kask/docs/reference/agent-trap-history.md` —
  reference on demand, context-injected never again, binding nothing.
